1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
This is Rill, created by Shivam Gupta.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,320
A citizen notices a stream concern.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,120
A coordinator has several places
to check and just two hours.

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,520
How should that team decide what happens next?

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,500
This isolated demo contains six fictional
sites and synthetic observations.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,160
It shows the complete path from a
citizen report to an explained decision,

7
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,900
assigned work and a documented follow-up.

8
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:32,660
The observer chooses a monitored site,
records the time and describes what they

9
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:34,420
noticed: litter beside the stream.

10
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:37,520
The guidance keeps the
visit on a safe public path.

11
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,140
Recording the visible evidence does
not require guessing what caused it.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,340
A short field note adds context,
while uncertainty remains visible.

13
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:54,140
A photograph can support a human reviewer,
but Rill does not diagnose pollution

14
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:59,200
from an image. After a final review,
the report is submitted and retains its

15
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,380
synthetic label throughout this demonstration.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,340
Now the coordinator can inspect the evidence.

17
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,560
Verification priority and evidence
strength answer different questions.

18
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:14,560
The explanation shows the reported
signal, matching reports from other

19
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,100
accounts, public access,

20
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:18,260
recency and the information gap.

21
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,000
Rill shows its reasoning and
explains the next useful check.

22
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:26,260
This priority guides attention; it
is not a contamination probability.

23
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:34,380
Habitat, animal contact and human
use stay connected to the concern.

24
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:38,540
These contexts help the coordinator
decide what to investigate while keeping

25
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:39,620
uncertainty visible.

26
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:43,840
Here is Rill's signature
interaction: plan the next two hours.

27
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:47,300
Each visit includes an access
allowance and observation time.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,640
Reduce the budget to 60 minutes,
and the selection changes.

29
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:56,120
Limited time means choosing one visit.

30
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:00,980
Back at 120 minutes, three visits fit.

31
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,160
Another concern must wait.

32
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,380
Every deferral has a reason.

33
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:12,420
Restricted sites and serious signs
stay out of routine volunteer plans.

34
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:18,300
The coordinator confirms safe access and
assigns the work, with owners and due dates.

35
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:21,960
The coordinator opens the litter case,

36
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,640
reviews the report and records a decision.

37
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:28,000
The original evidence stays
connected to that judgment.

38
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,280
The verification task is started,
then completed with a result.

39
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:39,540
These findings belong to this fictional
scenario, with the responsible person recorded.

40
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,640
Recording a response still
does not close the case.

41
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:45,760
Resolution stays blocked until a later recheck.

42
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,040
The coordinator creates the follow-up task,

43
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:52,680
records the later finding and completes it.

44
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:57,900
The visible concern may have changed; water
quality and contact safety remain unknown.

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,900
The observation can now be resolved.

46
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:05,860
Rill shows a documented loop:
one report, a human decision,

47
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,580
completed verification and a later recheck.

48
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,380
The decision trail preserves authors,
times and the rule version used at

49
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:16,720
review. The retained record can be exported.

50
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:24,980
Choose JSON, CSV, GeoJSON or
an experimental FHIR R4 bundle.

51
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:28,720
This actual download contains
environmental locations,

52
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:32,620
observations, tasks and provenance.
It creates no patient records.

53
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:34,600
These measures track reviews,

54
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:37,260
completed tasks, rechecks and closed loops.

55
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:41,500
They show follow-through, without claiming
improved water quality or reduced health risk.

56
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:45,740
The next step is a supervised
pilot with monitoring coordinators.

57
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:50,480
Test time saved and documented follow-through,
with volunteers contributing free.

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,620
The core needs no paid AI key.

59
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,620
Every useful stream concern
deserves a visible, checked outcome.
